{"rule": "Return this JSON object with the rule field unchanged. The text field holds, in at most 1800 characters, everything a reader would need to know of the story so far, plus one new event that moves the story forward. It is written for a human reader.", "text": "At Vardnes the tide came in an hour early. Marit Solvang, forty-seven, saw the mussel bucket on the flat rock go under at 13:09, fifty-one minutes before the table's 14:00. The keeper, Anders Holm, had left the lighthouse on the second of March; his cottage key hung on a nail behind her door, and his logbook recorded the drift beginning the previous July: four minutes, then nine, then twenty-two. The ferry Kittiwake passed the point at 16:40 every Tuesday and Friday. Her brother Tomas worked at the fish plant in Bergen and phoned on Sundays. The cottage barometer had sat at 1003 for a week. With water at the shed doorstep she moved the outboard to the loft. Three gulls sat on the breakwater; a fourth lay dead on the shingle. Her father was Eirik; her grandmother Signe had kept the tide table for forty years. The village counted 212 people at the last census.

Before dark she walked to the cottage with the logbook. The shore path was flooded at the second bend, so she climbed through the heather and came down behind the house. The door was unlocked, the stove still warm. On the table lay a page in Holm's hand, dated that morning: 'Drift now 51 min. Light must be kept tonight. Do not wait for Tomas.' Beside it was a second key she had never seen, stamped 2, and the lamp room door at the top of the stairs stood open.

She climbed. The lamp was cold, its wick trimmed and fuel tank full, as if readied and left. Through the salt-streaked glass she saw, far out beyond the point, the Kittiwake riding at anchor where no ferry had ever stopped, dark except for a single white light at her bow. It was Thursday. Marit checked her watch: 15:52. As she watched, the light on the ferry went out, came on, went out again—three times, then a pause, then three more. She found the matches on the shelf and struck one."}

